# nethersec host scripts: environment for the detector, and the report transport for
# every host script. install.sh writes /etc/nethersec/report.env (root:root, mode 600)
# from this file and fills in the values you pass it; systemd loads it. Edit it by hand
# afterwards if you like. systemd reads this file itself (it strips quotes around a
# value); do not `source` it from a shell, which would mangle a value with shell
# characters in it.

# --- the report transport (install.sh --report-url / --format / --new-key) ---
# Where reports go. For a nethersec hub: https://<hub>/v1/report
NETHERSEC_REPORT_URL=
# hub (default) | discord | ntfy | json. A Discord webhook URL picks discord by itself.
#NETHERSEC_REPORT_FORMAT=hub
# This box's key. The hub format needs it; ntfy and json send it as a bearer token when
# set; Discord ignores it. Make one on the box with `install.sh --new-key`, which prints
# only the line for the hub's keys file. Never paste it on a command line.
NETHERSEC_REPORT_KEY=

# --- this box's source name (install.sh --source) ----------------------------
# a-z, 0-9 and '-', 1 to 40 characters, e.g. web1. It must match the source the key
# was made for.
HOSTSEC_SOURCE=

# --- optional ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Grouping label sent with every report (the hub can group cards by it).
#HOSTSEC_GROUP=

# Failed-login count (since the previous run, ~30 min) that triggers an SSH burst
# alert. Default 2000: a server on the internet sees hundreds to thousands of scanning
# attempts per window, so a low value just cries wolf. Tune per box.
#HOSTSEC_SSH_BURST=2000

# Severity of the SSH burst alert: critical (page) | warning (quiet card, DEFAULT) |
# info. On a box that takes SSH keys only, brute force cannot land, so a burst is noise
# worth a quiet card, not a page. Set critical only where password auth is enabled.
#HOSTSEC_SSH_SEVERITY=warning

# Log error-rate check: errors/HOUR across journald (priority err, minus the SSH
# scanner noise the burst check already owns) + docker container logs. Default
# threshold 600/hr; HIGH on purpose: app/container error logs are noisier than SSH
# auth, so a low value cries wolf. Baseline the box first, then lower it.
#HOSTSEC_LOGERR_BURST=600
# Severity of the log-error alert: critical (page) | warning (quiet card, DEFAULT) |
# info. Keep it warning until you know the box's normal error floor.
#HOSTSEC_LOGERR_SEVERITY=warning
# Set 0 to turn off just this check.
#HOSTSEC_LOGERR_ENABLE=1
# Extra grep -E alternation appended to the benign-noise exclusion for this box,
# e.g. a mail server: 'NOQUEUE:.*reject' ; a web box: ' 4[0-9][0-9] '
#HOSTSEC_LOGERR_EXCLUDE_RE=

# CVE-severity gate. A pending security update is surfaced ONLY when it fixes a HIGH or
# CRITICAL Ubuntu CVE: a stuck High/Critical pages and a cleared one posts a quiet
# "done" note; a stuck batch that is only low/medium raises ONE quiet card, while
# low/medium updates that auto-apply on schedule stay silent. Severity is looked up by
# cve-severity.py in Ubuntu's security notices (cached under /var/lib/nethersec,
# fail-safe: any lookup failure yields 'unknown'/'error', never a fabricated
# High/Critical or a false page). Set 0 on a box with no egress to ubuntu.com, or on a
# Debian or Raspberry Pi OS box: a stuck update then cards QUIET (no page).
#HOSTSEC_CVE_LOOKUP=1
# Hard outer timeout (seconds) on the lookup, on top of the helper's own per-request
# and whole-run budgets. Needs the `timeout` binary (coreutils).
#HOSTSEC_CVE_HARD_TIMEOUT=40

# Extra apt origins whose pending updates count as security updates, as an extended
# regex matched against apt's `Inst` line. On Raspberry Pi OS, whose archive ships
# Chromium, the kernel and libc6 from suite `stable`, not `-security`:
#HOSTSEC_SECURITY_ORIGIN_RE=Raspberry.Pi.Foundation:

# Hold a pending security update QUIET this long (seconds) before it can surface, so a
# routine daily auto-upgrade clears it first and only a STUCK update is ever evaluated
# (and then it pages ONLY if High/Critical). Default 93600 (26h) = one auto-upgrade
# window + margin. 0 = evaluate immediately.
#HOSTSEC_SECUPDATE_GRACE_SECS=93600

# Re-assert a still-raised condition at most this often (seconds). Default 6h.
#HOSTSEC_RESURFACE_SECS=21600

# Box drift checks. Livepatch and the IP-options drop are checked by default where
# /etc/os-release says Ubuntu (1 = always, 0 = never). The sshd check runs wherever sshd
# is installed.
#HOSTSEC_LIVEPATCH_CHECK=auto
#HOSTSEC_IPOPT_CHECK=auto
#HOSTSEC_SSHD_CHECK=1

# Sockets this box may listen on outside loopback. Any other one gets a quiet card.
# Unset = no listener check. Tokens are <proto>/<address>:<port> as `ss` prints them,
# with * for any address or any port, separated by spaces. Copy the line
# firewall-snapshot.sh prints, then swap a port that changes on every restart for *.
#HOSTSEC_LISTEN_ALLOW="tcp/0.0.0.0:22 tcp/[::]:22 tcp/*:443 udp/*:443"
